      "math_academy_id": "q-130735",
      "sequence_position": 15,
      "problem": "Suppose $B$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $det(B)=-5.$ What is the value of $det(3B)?$",
      "worked_solution": "Multiplying a $n\\times n$ matrix by a scalar $k$ is equivalent to multiplying each row by $k,$ and this occurs $n$ times. This leads to the result\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(k\\cdot B)={k}^{n}\\cdot det(B).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $k=3$ and $n=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(3B) & ={3}^{2}\\cdot det(B) \\\\  & =9\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =-45.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
